# Managed by envi (integrations/ssh/config). Included from ~/.ssh/config.
#
# SCOPE: THIS FILE APPLIES TO EVERY MACHINE
# =========================================
# It is versioned in the envi repo, which is public on GitHub and installed on all
# of the user's machines: the private laptop, the employer-owned work laptop, and
# the homelab nodes. `envi-update` fast-forwards it everywhere. There is no
# per-machine override layer for this file (only envi_env, envi_locations and
# envi_shortcuts have one), so anything added here cannot be opted out of locally.
#
# That makes it the wrong home for three kinds of setting, however tempting the
# proximity to the block below:
#
# - Anything naming a specific host, network or address. Private LAN addresses do
#   not belong in a public repo, and a host that is trusted from home is a
#   different host, or none at all, from the work laptop's network.
# - Anything granting trust to a target, `ForwardAgent` above all. A forwarded
#   agent lets root on the far end authenticate as the user for as long as the
#   connection is open. That is a per-machine, per-host decision.
# - Anything storing credentials, e.g. `UseKeychain`. Whether passphrases may go
#   into a given machine's keychain is that machine's owner's call, not this
#   repo's. If a macOS-only keyword is ever needed here anyway, it must be guarded
#   with `IgnoreUnknown`, or ssh breaks outright on the Linux nodes.
#
# Machine-specific settings go into the local ~/.ssh/config instead. envi only
# prepends its Include line there and leaves the rest of that file alone. Ordering
# works out because ssh takes the FIRST value obtained for a keyword and the block
# below claims only the three ControlMaster keywords, so later local blocks still
# win for everything else.
#
# Connection multiplexing (ControlMaster)
# =======================================
# The first interactive ssh to a host opens a long-lived "master" socket under
# ~/.ssh/cm/. Any later ssh to the SAME target (from any shell, e.g. an AI agent
# via `erun`) reuses that already-authenticated connection as a new channel, with
# no re-auth: no key, no password, no 2FA prompt.
#
# This is what lets an agent run commands on hosts YOU opened interactively,
# including hosts the agent has never seen and has no credentials for. You do the
# human auth once (password / 2FA / jump host); the agent piggybacks on it.
#
# - ControlPath uses %C (a hash of user+host+port), so the socket path never
#   exceeds the ~104-char unix-socket limit, even for long FQDNs or ProxyJump.
# - ControlPersist keeps the master alive for 30m after the last client exits,
#   so the agent can keep working briefly after you close the interactive pane.
Host *
    ControlMaster auto
    ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm/%C
    ControlPersist 30m
